# Stock: a chunked body trickled one framing unit at a time, and leaks nothing.
#
# Module-agnostic: every consumer has /__probe, and nginx/angie hold a request
# open while its body is still arriving, so these hold without depending on any
# consumer-specific content.
#
# HOW THIS DIFFERS FROM slowloris.rule. `send_slow <chunk> <ms>` dribbles the
# HEADER block in fixed-size byte pieces, so the server's header path is entered
# once per chunk. This file dribbles the BODY, and it cuts at chunked-framing
# boundaries rather than at fixed byte offsets: one complete
# `<hex>[;ext]\r\n<data>\r\n` unit per write. That is the difference the
# directive exists for -- a byte count lands mid size-line as often as on a
# boundary, so `send_slow` over a chunked body mostly exercises the server's
# partial-chunk-header path. `send_slow_chunks` exercises the one a real slow
# uploader produces: every read delivers a complete, valid, tiny chunk.
#
# HOW THIS DIFFERS FROM malformed-body.rule. That file puts hostile FRAMING on
# the wire (bare LF size lines, lengths beyond any real body) in a single write
# and asserts the worker survives parsing it. Every unit here is well-formed;
# the hostility is entirely in the ARRIVAL RATE. A module can parse malformed
# framing perfectly and still allocate once per read event, which is what this
# case is built to surface.
#
# WHAT THESE CASES ACTUALLY PROVE, AND WHAT THEY DO NOT. Read this before
# trusting a green run. The reference module answers `/__probe` for GET and HEAD
# only -- `ngx_http_test_ref_handler()` returns NGX_HTTP_NOT_ALLOWED at
# `t/module/ngx_http_test_ref_module.c:139`, which is BEFORE the
# `ngx_http_discard_request_body()` call at `:149`. So against the reference
# consumer a POST here is rejected on the method, and the trickled body is never
# handed to a body reader at all.
#
# That does not make the cases vacuous, but it does narrow them. What they cover
# against any consumer is the connection and harness side: the prober's own
# paced writer walks real framing and puts one complete unit per write on the
# wire, and the server survives a body arriving that way -- accepting the bytes,
# answering, and releasing every fd and pool block it took. A module that leaks
# per read event on the connection path still shows up in the deltas.
#
# The STRONGER claim -- that a per-READ rather than per-REQUEST allocation in a
# body READER is caught -- needs a location that actually reads the body. Point
# this file at a proxy_pass or return-after-body location on a consumer that has
# one, and the same deltas cover that path too. On `/__probe` they do not, and a
# green run here must not be read as evidence that they do.
#
# THE PACE IS DELIBERATELY MODEST. The interval only has to exceed the loopback
# round-trip to guarantee a separate read event per unit. It does NOT approach
# client_body_timeout: that value is consumer-configurable, and a rule assuming
# a default would fail on a conf that tuned it. This asserts that a slowly
# arriving body is served correctly, NOT that it is eventually cut off -- the
# timeout policy belongs to the consumer, not to the harness.
#
# The prober bounds a case's total stall (MAX_PAUSE_MS) and costs
# `send_slow_chunks` as if every unit were the smallest the wire allows (a
# zero-sized chunk, 5 bytes), so a pace slow enough to exceed that ceiling is
# rejected when the rule file loads rather than reported as a harness timeout
# later. The bodies here are far under it.
#

name              a chunked body trickled one unit at a time still completes
send              POST /__probe HTTP/1.1\r\n
send              Host: prober\r\n
send              Transfer-Encoding: chunked\r\n
send              Connection: close\r\n\r\n
send_slow_chunks  20
send              1\r\nA\r\n1\r\nB\r\n1\r\nC\r\n1\r\nD\r\n
send              1\r\nE\r\n1\r\nF\r\n1\r\nG\r\n1\r\nH\r\n
send              0\r\n\r\n
delta             fds == 0
delta             pool.cycle_used == 0
delta             pool.cycle_large == 0

# A size line carrying a chunk extension is one unit like any other -- the
# framing walk skips the extension to find the CRLF, so the pacing boundary is
# still the end of the data CRLF and not the semicolon. Worth its own case
# because an extension is the one legal way a size line can be arbitrarily long,
# which is exactly what makes the load-time cost estimate a floor rather than an
# exact count.
name              chunk extensions do not disturb the framing walk
send              POST /__probe HTTP/1.1\r\n
send              Host: prober\r\n
send              Transfer-Encoding: chunked\r\n
send              Connection: close\r\n\r\n
send_slow_chunks  20
send              2;a=1\r\nhi\r\n2;b=2\r\nyo\r\n0\r\n\r\n
delta             fds == 0
delta             pool.cycle_used == 0
delta             pool.cycle_large == 0

# The 5-byte zero-sized chunk is what the load-time cost estimate is charged at
# (MIN_CHUNK_UNIT_BYTES), so it is worth one case of its own -- but a RUN of them
# is not a thing a valid request can contain. Per RFC 9112 the first `0\r\n\r\n`
# ENDS the body; anything after it is trailer or the next message, not a fourth
# unit. The smallest legal non-terminal unit is `1\r\nX\r\n`, six bytes. So the
# floor the estimator uses is real but reachable only once per body, which is
# exactly why costing at it overestimates and is the safe direction.
#
# This case is therefore the minimum-width body: two 6-byte units and the 5-byte
# terminator, three writes, the smallest multi-unit walk the pacer can perform.
name              a minimum-width chunked body is still one unit per write
send              POST /__probe HTTP/1.1\r\n
send              Host: prober\r\n
send              Transfer-Encoding: chunked\r\n
send              Connection: close\r\n\r\n
send_slow_chunks  20
send              1\r\nX\r\n1\r\nY\r\n0\r\n\r\n
delta             fds == 0
delta             pool.cycle_used == 0
delta             pool.cycle_large == 0

name              worker survives the chunked-trickle battery
send              GET /__probe HTTP/1.1\r\n
send              Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect            status=200
probe             zone.present == true
delta             fds == 0
delta             pool.cycle_used == 0
delta             pool.cycle_large == 0
